A||N||7||rockcrawl||History of Mecham???||||5||
Z||000000||rockcrawl||01-31-2001||09:27 PM||||Well, I just picked up a set of Mecham side scoops. I was hoping someone could give me a quick history lesson about Mecham and thier dealings with Fieros. Who or what is Mecham? The quality of the urethane scoops is superb, they look as if they were made at the GM factory using the same machine. The tooling alone must have cost a fortune. I was wondering if anyone knows where they were made. Did Mecham have a contract with GM to make them?? When were they last available and how many were made? Any info would be greatly appreciated. I'm not sure why, but I'm extremely curious about these.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||stimpy||02-01-2001||12:20 AM||||Stupid double post!<p>[This message has been edited by stimpy (edited 02-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||stimpy||02-01-2001||12:20 AM||||I suspect that Mecham did have a pretty close relationship with GM. I remember in 85, you could bet Mecham special edition Firebirds from the dealer. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||02-01-2001||12:32 AM||||I think Meachum was part of GM's Racing division. They were the ones that set-up or assisted GM sponsored race cars. They had a lot to do with Fiero's and F-bodies. From what I remember they were more into body/chassis side of the racecars. This is all an assumption based on rumors I have heard over the years.||||reg||1
Z||000004||RossT||02-01-2001||12:33 AM||||I lived in Arizon during the late 80's and can remember we kicked our govenor "Mecham" out of office.  Its my understanding that he was related to the Mecham racing stuff going on in Californina at that time.  Thats all I know.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Wannabe||02-01-2001||05:16 PM||||Mecham first appeared in the late seventies tuning Trans Ams and Firebirds. If I recall correctly, he was the son of a Pontiac dealership owner. The 1978 Mecham Trans Am, with some mild engine and exhaust tuning on the 400 Pontiac engine ran in the low 14's in the quarter. Unheard of for any other car you could buy from a dealership in the late Seventies. There was also a special L82 Corvette available with a DNE five speed transmission. He continued to do sepcial "tuner" cars into the eightees and then sort of faded away.||||reg||1
Z||000006||rockcrawl||02-01-2001||06:14 PM||||I found this site on a search <A HREF="http://www.mechamperformance.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.mechamperformance.com/</A>  I don't know if it's the same Mecham, but I bet there is some relation. Glendale Arizona. <P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Pontiaddict||02-01-2001||07:13 PM||||In the late 70's. Dennis and Kyle Mecham were responsible for DKM Macho T/As among other things. The T/As could be ordered with turbocharged 400s in them. In the 80's The 3rd gen Firebirds could be had with various levels of performance upgrades ranging from a new carb, and headers trown into the trunk for you to install later, to (for export only) twin turbo chevy 400s. They also had suspension upgrades and a sweet rear wing that I wish I had. They sort of faded out during the late '80s and made a comeback building new Firebirds and Grand Prixs and such. Until recently, I didn't even know that they did anything for Fieros so I can't give you any info.||||reg||1
